    const Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content: 'Critical distinctions and assessments in post-amputation pain:\n- **Phantom limb pain (PLP)**: Pain perceived in the amputated limb. Described as burning, cramping, shooting, stabbing, or twisting. Present in 50-80% of amputees. Onset usually within the first week but can occur months later.\n- **Phantom limb sensation (PLS)**: Non-painful awareness of the missing limb (tingling, itching, sense of position). Present in nearly all amputees. Telescoping (perception that the phantom limb is shrinking) is common.\n- **Residual limb pain (RLP)**: Pain in the remaining stump. Causes include neuroma, bone spur, skin breakdown, infection, ischemia, heterotopic ossification, and poorly fitting prosthetic socket.\n- **Differentiation is essential**: Ask specifically where the pain is — in the remaining limb or in the missing part? What is the quality?\n- **Pre-amputation pain history**: Patients with severe pre-amputation pain are at higher risk for PLP (pain memory imprinting theory).\n- **Vascular status**: Assess contralateral limb viability. Check for signs of progressive PVD (claudication, rest pain, skin changes in the remaining foot). ABI of contralateral limb.\n- **Diabetes management**: Current HbA1c, glucose control, neuropathy symptoms in the remaining foot.\n- **Psychosocial assessment**: Screen for depression (PHQ-9), PTSD, grief/loss, social isolation, body image disturbance. Amputees have a 2-3x higher rate of depression than the general population.\n- **Functional goals**: Ambulatory expectations (K-level classification), prior mobility level, home accessibility, social support system.',
