    const Section(
      title: '+ Explain the neurophysiologic mechanisms of phantom limb pain and differentiate it from neuroma-related residual limb pain in this patient.',
      content: '**Mechanisms of Phantom Limb Pain**:\n\n1. **Peripheral mechanisms**:\n- After nerve transection, the proximal nerve stump forms a **neuroma** — a disorganized tangle of regenerating axons. These generate spontaneous ectopic discharges (abnormal firing without stimulation).\n- Upregulation of sodium channels (Nav1.3, Nav1.8) in the dorsal root ganglion increases neuronal excitability.\n- This patient\'s positive Tinel sign at the fibular transection site suggests a clinically significant neuroma contributing to both RLP and PLP.\n\n2. **Spinal cord mechanisms**:\n- Loss of afferent input from the amputated limb causes **dorsal horn reorganization**. Wide dynamic range neurons become hyperexcitable.\n- Loss of inhibitory interneuron activity leads to central sensitization at the spinal level.\n- Wind-up phenomenon: repeated C-fiber input from the neuroma amplifies spinal cord responses.\n\n3. **Cortical reorganization (most important)**:\n- The somatosensory cortex undergoes **maladaptive neuroplasticity**. The cortical area that previously represented the amputated foot is invaded by adjacent representations (e.g., the residual limb, genitals per Penfield homunculus).\n- **Ramachandran\'s theory**: The degree of cortical reorganization correlates with the intensity of phantom limb pain. The brain \"searches\" for the missing limb\'s input and misinterprets adjacent signals as pain.\n- **Neuromatrix theory (Melzack)**: The brain contains an innate body schema (neuromatrix) that generates a perception of the body independent of peripheral input. When the expected input is absent, the neuromatrix generates abnormal sensory experiences including pain.\n\n4. **Pain memory**: Pre-amputation pain patterns can be \"imprinted\" in the CNS and replayed as phantom pain. This patient had chronic diabetic foot pain before amputation, which may be contributing.\n\n**Differentiating PLP from Neuroma-Related RLP**:\n- The **burning and cramping in the phantom foot** = phantom limb pain (cortical and spinal mechanisms)\n- The **point tenderness with positive Tinel sign at the fibular transection site** = residual limb pain from neuroma (peripheral mechanism)\n- These often coexist and both require treatment. The neuroma is likely driving peripheral input that maintains central sensitization.',
