      content: '**Diagnostic Studies**:\n\n1. **Ultrasound of the residual limb**: High-resolution ultrasound can identify neuroma formation at the nerve transection sites. Neuromas appear as hypoechoic bulbous enlargements of the nerve stump. This is the preferred first-line imaging modality.\n\n2. **Diagnostic nerve block**: Lidocaine injection at the site of the suspected fibular neuroma. If it eliminates both the local tenderness AND reduces the phantom pain, this confirms the neuroma as a significant peripheral driver of the PLP. If phantom pain persists despite block, central mechanisms predominate.\n\n3. **MRI of the residual limb**: If ultrasound is inconclusive, MRI can identify neuroma, heterotopic ossification, abscess, or bone spur. Also assess for residual osteomyelitis given diabetic history.\n\n4. **Vascular studies**: Arterial duplex of the residual limb to ensure adequate perfusion for healing and prosthetic use. ABI of the contralateral limb to assess progression of PVD.\n\n5. **X-ray of the residual limb**: Rule out bone spur, heterotopic ossification, or retained foreign body.\n\n6. **Do NOT order**: EMG/NCS of the residual limb is not helpful for neuroma diagnosis and is unnecessarily painful. Brain imaging is not indicated for phantom limb pain in the absence of other neurologic findings.',
