      content: '**Pharmacologic Management**:\n\n1. **First-line — Gabapentinoids**:\n- **Gabapentin**: Start 300 mg at bedtime, titrate by 300 mg every 3-5 days to target dose of 900-1800 mg TID. Address his sleep disruption and neuropathic pain simultaneously.\n- **Pregabalin**: Alternative at 75 mg BID, titrate to 150-300 mg BID. Better bioavailability and less variable absorption than gabapentin.\n\n2. **Adjunctive — Tricyclic Antidepressant or SNRI**:\n- **Nortriptyline 25-75 mg at bedtime**: Effective for neuropathic pain and sleep, lower anticholinergic side effects than amitriptyline. However, use with caution given his CAD history (check baseline ECG for QTc).\n- **Duloxetine 60 mg daily**: Safer cardiovascular profile, also addresses depressive symptoms.\n\n3. **Topical agents for residual limb**:\n- **Lidocaine 5% patch**: Apply over the neuroma site for 12 hours on/12 hours off.\n- **Capsaicin 8% patch**: Single application by a trained provider, can provide 3 months of relief by desensitizing peripheral nociceptors.\n\n4. **Avoid**: Opioids are generally not effective for phantom limb pain and carry significant risks in this population.\n\n**Non-Pharmacologic Interventions**:\n\n1. **Mirror therapy** (strongest evidence for PLP):\n- Patient places the intact left foot in front of a mirror positioned at midline. Observing the mirror reflection moving creates a visual illusion of the missing right foot moving. This \"fools\" the cortex into receiving the expected visual-proprioceptive feedback, reducing maladaptive cortical reorganization.\n- Protocol: 15-20 minutes daily for a minimum of 4 weeks. Home mirror box can be constructed inexpensively.\n\n2. **Graded motor imagery (GMI)**: Three-stage approach — laterality recognition, explicit motor imagery (imagining moving the phantom limb), then mirror therapy. Addresses cortical reorganization systematically.\n\n3. **TENS (Transcutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation)**: Apply electrodes to the residual limb. Provides competing sensory input to modulate spinal gating. Use conventional high-frequency (80-100 Hz) TENS for 30-60 minutes, 2-3 times daily.\n\n4. **Desensitization program**: Progressive tactile stimulation of the residual limb (tapping, rubbing with various textures, vibration) to normalize sensory input and reduce allodynia.\n\n5. **Prosthetic fitting**: Wearing a well-fitting prosthesis itself reduces PLP in many patients by providing proprioceptive feedback to the cortex. Prioritize prosthetic training once pain is manageable.\n\n**Interventional Options** (if conservative measures fail):\n\n6. **Targeted neuroma injection**: Corticosteroid + local anesthetic injection at the fibular neuroma under ultrasound guidance.\n\n7. **Peripheral nerve stimulation**: Implanted electrode adjacent to the nerve proximal to the neuroma.\n\n8. **Targeted Muscle Reinnervation (TMR)**: Surgical procedure that transfers the severed nerve endings to nearby motor nerve branches, giving them a target to innervate. This prevents neuroma formation and has strong emerging evidence for both treatment and prevention of PLP.\n\n9. **Dorsal root ganglion (DRG) stimulation**: Neuromodulation targeting the DRG at the relevant spinal levels for refractory cases.',
