      title: '+ Describe the prosthetic prescription process, K-level classification, and insurance coverage considerations. If this patient were a veteran, what additional resources would be available?',
      content: '**Prosthetic Prescription Process**:\n\n1. **Physician prescription**: The physiatrist provides a detailed prescription including the type of prosthesis and specific componentry. The prescription must be based on the patient\'s functional potential, not just current ability.\n\n2. **K-Level Classification (Medicare Functional Classification Level)**:\n- **K0**: Does not have the ability or potential to ambulate or transfer. Prosthesis does not enhance quality of life (no prosthesis justified).\n- **K1**: Has the ability or potential to use a prosthesis for transfers or household ambulation on level surfaces (limited household ambulator).\n- **K2**: Has the ability or potential for limited community ambulation (community ambulator with environmental barriers).\n- **K3**: Has the ability or potential for ambulation with variable cadence, including community activities beyond simple locomotion (active community ambulator).\n- **K4**: Has the ability or potential for prosthetic ambulation that exceeds basic ambulation, including high-impact activities (athlete, active adult).\n\nThis patient\'s K-level: Given his PVD, diabetes, CAD, and age, he is likely a **K2 ambulator** with potential for K3 depending on rehabilitation progress.\n\n3. **Componentry based on K-level**:\n- K2 transtibial: PTB or TSB socket, pelite liner or gel liner with pin lock, SACH or single-axis foot, endoskeletal pylon.\n- If he demonstrates K3 potential: dynamic energy-storing foot (e.g., Flex-Foot) may be justified.\n\n**Medicare Part B Coverage**:\n- Medicare covers **80%** of the approved prosthetic device. The patient is responsible for the 20% coinsurance (or supplemental insurance covers it).\n- **Requires**: Physician prescription with documented medical necessity, K-level justification, prosthetist evaluation.\n- **Prior authorization** may be required for higher-cost components.\n- Medicare covers **5 prosthetic socks** per year and socket replacements as needed with documentation of poor fit.\n- Repair and replacement are covered when medically necessary.\n\n**VA Benefits (if veteran)**:\nIf this patient were a service-connected veteran:\n1. **VA Prosthetics and Sensory Aids Service**: Provides prosthetic devices at no cost for service-connected conditions.\n2. **No K-level restrictions**: The VA is generally more generous than Medicare in prosthetic componentry. Veterans may receive microprocessor-controlled knees, specialized sport prostheses, and multiple prostheses for different activities.\n3. **Amputation System of Care**: Specialized VA Regional Amputation Centers with dedicated interdisciplinary teams.\n4. **Lifetime prosthetic care**: Ongoing replacements, repairs, and upgrades at no cost.\n5. **Additional benefits**: Adaptive housing grants, automobile adaptation grants, clothing allowance for prosthesis-related wear, caregiver support.\n6. **Peer support**: VA-sponsored peer visitation programs connecting new amputees with experienced amputee mentors.',
